---
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import { links } from '../config/site';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is “cremation insurance” a special kind of policy?',
    a: '<p>No. It’s a name for final expense insurance: a small whole life policy chosen to cover a cremation and final bills. The policy pays your beneficiary in cash, and they decide how to use it.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much coverage do I need for a cremation?',
    a: '<p>Start with the cremation you want, then add final bills. A direct cremation costs much less than a funeral with a viewing. A service, an urn, a niche or a plot for the ashes all add to the total. Ask two or three providers for their price lists and choose an amount that covers your plan with a cushion.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the money have to go to a certain funeral home?',
    a: '<p>No. Life insurance pays your beneficiary, not a funeral home, so your family can choose any provider, and can change plans if prices or wishes change. A preneed contract is different: it’s an agreement with one provider.</p>',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I get coverage if I have health problems?',
    a: '<p>Often, yes. Many insurers accept conditions that are under control. If the health questions rule out a full-coverage plan, a graded or guaranteed issue plan may still be available, with a waiting period before the full amount is paid for death from natural causes. <a href="/guaranteed-issue-vs-simplified-issue/">How the plan types work</a>.</p>',
  },
];
---
<Guide
  title="Cremation Insurance in Florida: What It Covers"
  h1="Cremation insurance in Florida"
  description="How to pay for a cremation in Florida with final expense insurance: what cremation costs, how much coverage to choose, and what Florida requires."
  path="/cremation-insurance-florida/"
  crumb="Cremation insurance in Florida"
  eyebrow="Cremation"
  lede="Most American families now choose cremation. A small life insurance policy can pay for it, so the cost and the decisions don't land on your family."
  summary={[
    "“Cremation insurance” is final expense whole life insurance sized for a cremation and final bills.",
    "The policy pays your beneficiary in cash, so your family can use any funeral home or crematory.",
    "A direct cremation costs far less than a funeral with a viewing, so many people need less coverage.",
    "No medical exam. Approval depends on your answers to a few health questions.",
    "Florida requires a signed authorization and a medical examiner's approval before any cremation.",
  ]}
>
  <h2>Cremation is now the most common choice</h2>
  <p>
    About 62% of Americans who died in 2024 were cremated, according to the Cremation Association of North America, and the share keeps
    growing. Families choose cremation for many reasons: a lower cost, more freedom about when and where to hold a service, relatives who
    live in other states, or simply personal preference.
  </p>
  <p>A cremation costs less than a traditional funeral with burial, but it still costs money, and someone has to pay it quickly.</p>

  <h2>What a cremation costs</h2>
  <p>
    Prices vary a lot between providers, and your choices matter more than anything else. The National Funeral Directors Association's 2023
    price study put the national median for a funeral with a viewing and cremation at about $6,280. A direct cremation, with no viewing or
    service beforehand, usually costs much less: see <a href="/florida-funeral-costs/">published prices in Orlando, Tampa, Jacksonville and
    Miami</a>. These can add to the total:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A viewing, memorial service or celebration of life</li>
    <li>An urn, or a keepsake for each family member</li>
    <li>A niche in a columbarium, a cemetery plot for the ashes, or scattering arrangements</li>
    <li>Certified death certificates, an obituary, flowers and travel for family</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout">
    <h3>Your rights when you compare prices</h3>
    <p>
      Under the Federal Trade Commission's Funeral Rule, funeral homes must give you an itemized price list, and tell you prices over the
      phone if you ask. You don't have to buy a casket for a cremation; a simple alternative container is allowed.
      <a href={links.ftcFuneralRule} rel="noopener">Read the FTC Funeral Rule</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>How much coverage to choose</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Price the cremation you want.</strong> Ask two or three providers near you for their general price list.</li>
    <li><strong>Add the extras and final bills.</strong> A service, an urn or niche, plus any medical bills, credit cards or the last month's rent.</li>
    <li><strong>Leave a cushion.</strong> Prices rise over time, so most families round up.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Final expense plans usually range from about $5,000 to $25,000 of coverage. Because a cremation can cost less than a burial, many people
    choose an amount toward the lower end, which keeps the monthly premium down. <a href="/final-expense-insurance-cost/">See what changes the price</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Life insurance or a preneed cremation plan?</h2>
  <p>
    Some funeral homes and crematories sell preneed contracts: you choose and pay for the arrangements ahead of time with that provider. In
    Florida these contracts are regulated by the Department of Financial Services under Chapter 497 of the Florida Statutes. Life insurance
    works differently:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="stack">
      <caption>Final expense life insurance compared with a preneed contract</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Final expense life insurance</th><th scope="col">Preneed contract</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Who gets the money</th><td data-label="Life insurance">Your beneficiary, in cash</td><td data-label="Preneed contract">The provider named in the contract</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Choice of provider</th><td data-label="Life insurance">Any funeral home or crematory</td><td data-label="Preneed contract">That provider; moving it depends on the contract</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Money left over</th><td data-label="Life insurance">Goes to your beneficiary</td><td data-label="Preneed contract">Depends on the contract</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Other bills</th><td data-label="Life insurance">Can also cover medical bills or debts</td><td data-label="Preneed contract">Covers the goods and services listed</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Some families use both. The right mix depends on how settled your plans are.</p>

  <h2>What Florida requires before a cremation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A signed authorization.</strong> The legally authorized person, usually a person you named, your spouse or your next of kin in
      an order set by law, must sign before a cremation can happen.
    </li>
    <li><strong>A medical examiner's approval.</strong> In Florida, the district medical examiner reviews the death certificate and must approve before a cremation.</li>
    <li><strong>Your wishes, in writing.</strong> Writing down that you want cremation, and what should happen to the ashes, prevents disagreements later.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Because of these steps, a cremation usually takes several days to arrange. The funeral home or crematory handles the paperwork.</p>

  <h2>Applying is simple</h2>
  <p>
    No medical exam. Approval depends on your answers to a few health questions, and many insurers give a decision during the phone call.
    Coverage starts once the insurer approves the application and the first premium is paid. If the health questions rule out a
    full-coverage plan, a graded or guaranteed issue plan may still be available, with a waiting period before the full amount is paid for
    death from natural causes.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions about cremation coverage</h2>
  <Faq items={faqs} />
</Guide>
